CABLELESS ENERGY INFRASTRUCTURE: 

A Distributed Architecture for Addressable, Stored, Routable, and Safety-Governed Wireless Power Delivery

ouadi Maakoul 

AI : chatGpt, Deepseek 

Abstract

This dissertation investigates the conditions under which a significant fraction of local low-voltage electrical distribution can be virtualized into a controlled electromagnetic energy fabric. We propose the Cableless Energy Distribution Architecture (CEDA), a building-scale, multi-layer infrastructure that integrates local energy storage, heterogeneous wireless power transfer (WPT) modalities, spatial energy addressing, multi-receiver power allocation, device authentication, electromagnetic safety control, and intelligent energy routing. We formalize the mathematical foundations of CEDA through coupled-mode theory for near-field resonant transfer, radiative power transfer models for far-field operation, and a constrained multi-objective optimization framework for heterogeneous multi-receiver allocation. We derive the fundamental bounds on spatial energy addressing under IEC/IEEE 63184:2025 and IEC TR 63377:2022 assessment frameworks and establish a safety-governed control plane that dynamically modulates power delivery in the presence of biological tissues and implantable cardiac devices. Rather than asserting a fixed virtualization fraction, we formulate the achievable virtualization as a constrained optimization problem over load serviceability, safety envelopes, and economic parameters. CEDA incorporates an exposure-assessment framework consistent with IEC/IEEE 63184:2025 for inductive WPT operating within 3 kHz–30 MHz, and with IEC TR 63377:2022 for radiative WPT operating within 30 MHz–300 GHz; it does not claim compliance with standards outside their stated scope. The principal contribution is not a new physical transfer mechanism but a system-level architecture that treats electrical energy as a routable, addressable, authenticated, and safety-governed resource across heterogeneous wireless modalities.

Keywords: wireless power transfer, distributed energy storage, spatial power routing, electromagnetic safety, multi-receiver allocation, energy addressing, building-scale infrastructure, SAR assessment, safe energy routing.

