2. Mathematical Foundations

2.1 Coupled-Mode Theory for Multi-Receiver Resonant WPT

We model a resonant WPT system with N_T transmitters and N_R receivers as a set of coupled resonant circuits. Let \mathbf{x}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^{2(N_T + N_R)} denote the state vector of currents and voltages. The coupled-mode equations in the frequency domain are:

\begin{pmatrix}
\mathbf{Z}_T & j\omega\mathbf{M}_{TR} \\
j\omega\mathbf{M}_{TR}^\top & \mathbf{Z}_R
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
\mathbf{I}_T \\
\mathbf{I}_R
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
\mathbf{V}_s \\
\mathbf{0}
\end{pmatrix}

where \mathbf{Z}_T \in \mathbb{C}^{N_T \times N_T} and \mathbf{Z}_R \in \mathbb{C}^{N_R \times N_R} are the transmitter and receiver impedance matrices including self-impedance and compensation, \mathbf{M}_{TR} \in \mathbb{R}^{N_T \times N_R} is the mutual inductance matrix with elements M_{ij} depending on relative position and orientation, \mathbf{I}_T and \mathbf{I}_R are the current phasors, and \mathbf{V}_s is the source voltage vector.

Remark 2.1 (Multi-Receiver Coupling). The receivers are not independent. The mutual coupling between receivers, represented by off-diagonal terms in \mathbf{Z}_R, introduces cross-coupling that modifies the effective load seen by each receiver. This effect must be included in any rigorous multi-receiver analysis.

For a single transmitter–receiver pair with self-inductances L_1, L_2, mutual inductance M, and compensation capacitances C_1, C_2, the coupling coefficient is:

k = \frac{M}{\sqrt{L_1 L_2}}

The coil-to-coil transfer efficiency at resonance is:

\eta_{\text{coil}} = \frac{k^2 Q_t Q_r}{\left(1 + \sqrt{1 + k^2 Q_t Q_r}\right)^2}

where Q_t = \omega L_1 / R_1 and Q_r = \omega L_2 / R_2 are the quality factors. For k^2 Q_t Q_r \gg 1, \eta_{\text{coil}} \to 1 asymptotically, but practical constraints on Q and k limit achievable efficiency. The system-level efficiency is the cascade:

\eta_{\text{system}} = \eta_{\text{inverter}} \cdot \eta_{\text{coil}} \cdot \eta_{\text{rectifier}} \cdot \eta_{\text{DC-DC}}

For SAE J2954, the regulated grid-to-battery efficiency is \eta_{\text{system}} \geq 0.85 at nominal alignment. We adopt this benchmark as the reference efficiency for resonant-mode operation in CEDA.

Proposition 2.1 (Multi-Receiver Power Allocation Convexity). Consider the power allocation problem:

\max_{\{\mathbf{I}_T\}} \sum_{r=1}^{N_R} w_r P_r

subject to

P_r \geq P_{\min}^{(r)} \quad \forall r

\|\mathbf{I}_T^{(n)}\| \leq I_{\max} \quad \forall n

\sum_{n} P_{\text{Tx}}^{(n)} \leq P_{\text{budget}}

where P_r = \frac{1}{2}\text{Re}\left(\mathbf{I}_r^H \mathbf{Z}_r \mathbf{I}_r\right) and the coupling is fixed. Under the assumption of linear receiver loads and fixed mutual inductance matrix \mathbf{M}_{TR}, the feasible set is convex and the objective is concave in \mathbf{I}_T when the receiver currents are eliminated via the impedance equation. The proof follows from the positive semidefiniteness of the Schur complement of the coupled impedance matrix.

Remark 2.2. For nonlinear rectenna loads, the problem becomes non-convex. We adopt successive convex approximation (SCA) as the solution method, with convergence guarantees under mild regularity conditions [14].

2.2 Radiative WPT and Friis-Based Power Transfer

For far-field RF WPT, the received power at distance d from a transmitter with effective isotropic radiated power P_{\text{EIRP}} and receiver antenna gain G_r is given by the Friis transmission equation:

P_r(d) = P_{\text{EIRP}} \cdot G_r \cdot \left(\frac{\lambda}{4\pi d}\right)^2

where \lambda = c / f is the wavelength. The power conversion efficiency (PCE) of the rectenna further reduces the DC output power:

P_{\text{DC}} = \eta_{\text{rect}}(P_r) \cdot P_r

Recent rectenna designs achieve 80% RF-to-DC conversion efficiency at 2.45 GHz under 10 dBm input power, with efficiency maintained above 60% across a wide load range. For beamforming systems with N_T antennas, the array gain is:

G_{\text{array}} = N_T \cdot G_{\text{element}}

and the beam is steered toward a receiver at angular position (\theta, \phi) by applying phase shifts:

\phi_n = -\frac{2\pi}{\lambda} \mathbf{r}_n \cdot \hat{\mathbf{u}}(\theta, \phi)

Remark 2.3 (Mutual Coupling in Arrays). The array gain formula neglects mutual coupling between antenna elements. For dense arrays, the effective gain is reduced by a coupling factor \Gamma_{\text{cpl}}, and the beamforming weights must be adjusted accordingly. We include this as a correction term in numerical simulations.

2.3 Spatial Energy Addressing and Routing

We define an energy address as a tuple:

\mathcal{A} = \left( \text{ID}_r, \mathbf{p}_r, P_{\text{req}}, \mathcal{P}, \tau, \mathcal{S}_r, \mathcal{D}_r \right)

where \text{ID}_r is the receiver identifier, \mathbf{p}_r \in \mathbb{R}^3 is the spatial position, P_{\text{req}} is the requested power, \mathcal{P} is the priority class, \tau is the requested duration, \mathcal{S}_r is the safety state of the receiver environment, and \mathcal{D}_r is the device class (including medical device status).

The energy routing problem is to determine a set of transmitter excitations \{\mathbf{I}_T^{(n)}\} and a time schedule \{t_k\} such that:

\min_{\mathbf{I}_T, \{t_k\}} \sum_{r} \left( P_{\text{req}}^{(r)} - P_{\text{del}}^{(r)} \right)^2

subject to:

P_{\text{del}}^{(r)} \leq P_{\max}^{(r)} \quad \forall r

\mathcal{S}_{\text{safe}}(f, \mathbf{x}, t, \mathcal{H}, \mathcal{D}) \leq \mathbf{0} \quad \forall \mathbf{x} \in \Omega_{\text{human}}, \forall t

\|\mathbf{I}_T^{(n)}\| \leq I_{\max} \quad \forall n

\sum_{n} P_{\text{Tx}}^{(n)} \leq P_{\text{budget}}

where \mathcal{S}_{\text{safe}} is the safety constraint vector defined in Section 2.4.

Remark 2.4 (Energy as a Network Resource). The routing formulation treats energy analogously to a network flow: each receiver has an address, each transmitter has a capacity, and the allocation problem minimizes the mismatch between requested and delivered power subject to safety and physical constraints. This is distinct from traditional WPT, which optimizes point-to-point efficiency. The network abstraction enables scheduling, prioritization, and authentication that are absent from conventional WPT systems.

2.4 Safety Constraint Vector Formalism

We reject the reduction of electromagnetic safety to a single SAR limit. Instead, we define a safety constraint vector:

\mathcal{S}_{\text{safe}}(f, \mathbf{x}, t, \mathcal{H}, \mathcal{D}) =
\begin{pmatrix}
\text{SAR}(f, \mathbf{x}, t) - \text{SAR}_{\lim}(f, \mathcal{H}) \\
|\mathbf{E}_{\text{int}}(f, \mathbf{x}, t)| - E_{\lim}(f) \\
|\mathbf{J}(f, \mathbf{x}, t)| - J_{\lim}(f) \\
B_{\text{ext}}(f, \mathbf{x}, t) - B_{\text{CIED}} \\
T(\mathbf{x}, t) - T_{\text{safe}} \\
P_{\text{contact}}(t) - P_{\text{contact,lim}}
\end{pmatrix}
\leq \mathbf{0}

where:

· \text{SAR}(f, \mathbf{x}, t) is the specific absorption rate at position \mathbf{x} and time t;
· \mathcal{H} is the human occupancy state;
· \mathcal{D} is the device presence state, including CIEDs;
· \text{SAR}_{\lim}(f, \mathcal{H}) is the frequency-dependent and occupancy-dependent SAR limit;
· B_{\text{CIED}} is the magnetic field threshold for cardiac implantable electronic device immunity, consistent with ISO 14117;
· T(\mathbf{x}, t) is the local temperature;
· P_{\text{contact}} is the contact current.

The regulatory limits are not a single number. ICNIRP 2020 specifies a peak spatial-averaged 10 g SAR limit of 2 W/kg for general public exposure between 100 kHz and 6 GHz, with additional restrictions for whole-body exposure and limb exposure. IEEE C95.1-2019 covers 0 Hz to 300 GHz and explicitly states that its exposure limits “might not be protective with respect to the use of medical devices or implants”. IEC/IEEE 63184:2025 specifies assessment methods for inductive WPT in the range 3 kHz–30 MHz and explicitly states that it “does not consider the immunity of cardiac implantable electrical devices to radiated disturbances from WPT systems”.

Definition 2.1 (Safety Envelope). The safety envelope \mathcal{S}_{\text{safe}} is the set of states \mathbf{s}(t) satisfying all constraints in the safety vector:

\mathcal{S}_{\text{safe}} = \left\{ \mathbf{s} \in \mathbb{R}^n : \mathcal{S}_{\text{safe}}(f, \mathbf{x}, t, \mathcal{H}, \mathcal{D}) \leq \mathbf{0} \right\}

The control objective is not merely to avoid violating the envelope, but to ensure that the probability of future violation remains below a threshold:

\Pr \left[ \mathbf{s}(t + \Delta t) \notin \mathcal{S}_{\text{safe}} \mid \mathbf{s}(t), u(t) \right] \leq \epsilon

where u(t) is the power control input and \epsilon is a small safety probability margin. This transforms safety from reactive constraint checking to predictive constraint enforcement.

2.5 Virtualization Fraction as an Optimization Problem

We define the virtualization fraction \alpha not as a fixed number but as the solution to an optimization problem over load serviceability.

Let \mathcal{L} be the set of loads in a building. Each load l \in \mathcal{L} has:

· Power demand P_l (peak) and energy demand E_l (daily);
· Spatial extent d_l (distance from nearest transmitter);
· Service criticality c_l \in [0,1];
· Temporal flexibility t_l;
· Safety constraints S_l (proximity to humans, medical devices, etc.).

For a given policy \pi (allocation, scheduling, mode selection), let v_l(\pi) \in [0,1] be the fraction of load l’s energy demand that can be served wirelessly under policy \pi. The virtualization fraction is:

\alpha^\star = \max_{\pi \in \Pi} \frac{\sum_{l \in \mathcal{L}} E_l \cdot v_l(\pi)}{\sum_{l \in \mathcal{L}} E_l}

subject to:

v_l(\pi) = 0 \quad \text{if } P_l > P_{\text{th}} \text{ or } d_l > d_{\text{th}} \text{ or } \mathcal{S}_{\text{safe}} \not\leq \mathbf{0}

v_l(\pi) \in [0,1] \quad \forall l

\sum_{l} P_{\text{del}, l}(\pi) \leq P_{\text{fabric}} \quad \forall t

The virtualization fraction is therefore a function of system parameters:

\alpha^\star = f\left( P_l, d_l, \eta_l, t_l, S_l, O_l, C_l, R_l \right)

where \eta_l is the end-to-end efficiency, O_l is occupancy, C_l is cost, and R_l is reliability.

Hypothesis 2.1. For a typical residential building with a hybrid cabled–wireless architecture and the parameter ranges specified in Section 4, \alpha^\star \in [0.15, 0.40] by energy, with the upper bound achieved only under favorable occupancy, safety, and cost conditions. This hypothesis is to be tested through simulation and, ultimately, experimental validation.

Remark 2.5. The previous draft’s claim of \alpha \approx 0.35–0.50 was by peak power. By energy, the fraction is lower because high-power, short-duration loads (cooking, heating) dominate peak demand but contribute a smaller share of daily energy than their peak suggests. A rigorous analysis must distinguish peak-power virtualization from energy virtualization.

