3. Architecture: The Cableless Energy Distribution Architecture (CEDA)

3.1 Overview and Definition of “Cableless”

Definition 3.1 (Cableless). Cableless does not mean conductor-free. CEDA virtualizes the terminal distribution of low-voltage electrical energy. A cabled backbone remains for high-power and long-distance transport. The architecture is:

\boxed{ \text{Grid/Backbone} \rightarrow \text{Local Storage} \rightarrow \text{Energy Fabric} \rightarrow \text{Wireless Receivers} }

CEDA is a six-layer reference architecture (Figure 1). Each layer is defined by its function, interfaces, and constraints.

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  LAYER 6: SAFETY PLANE                             │
│  SAR constraint vector · FOD · Human occupancy     │
│  · Medical device state · Thermal monitoring       │
│  · Predictive envelope enforcement · Emergency     │
│  shutdown                                          │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  LAYER 5: ENERGY CONTROL PLANE                     │
│  Discovery · Authentication · Localization         │
│  Power negotiation · Allocation · Routing          │
│  · Scheduling · Energy handshake                    │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  LAYER 4: ENERGY RECEIVERS (WER)                   │
│  Rectenna · Resonant coil · Rectifier · DC-DC      │
│  Authentication tag · Feedback channel              │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  LAYER 3: WIRELESS ENERGY FABRIC                   │
│  Inductive · Resonant · RF · Beamforming           │
│  · Optical · Metasurface-assist · Mode selection   │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  LAYER 2: LOCAL ENERGY STORAGE                     │
│  Battery · Supercapacitor · Hybrid storage         │
│  State-of-charge · Thermal management               │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  LAYER 1: ENERGY SOURCES                           │
│  Grid · Solar · Wind · Local generation            │
│  AC-DC conversion · Power factor correction        │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

Figure 1. The six-layer CEDA reference architecture.

3.2 Layer 1: Energy Sources

The source layer comprises grid connection, photovoltaic generation, and optionally wind or other local generation. The interface to Layer 2 is a DC bus at a nominal voltage V_{\text{bus}}. The energy balance equation is:

P_{\text{gen}}(t) + P_{\text{grid}}(t) - P_{\text{load}}(t) = \frac{dE_{\text{storage}}}{dt}

where P_{\text{gen}} is local generation, P_{\text{grid}} is grid import (positive) or export (negative), and P_{\text{load}} is total load including storage losses.

3.3 Layer 2: Local Energy Storage

Local storage decouples generation from consumption and provides the energy buffer for wireless transfer. The storage dynamics are:

\frac{dE_{\text{st}}}{dt} = \eta_{\text{ch}} P_{\text{ch}}(t) - \frac{1}{\eta_{\text{dis}}} P_{\text{dis}}(t)

where \eta_{\text{ch}} and \eta_{\text{dis}} are charge and discharge efficiencies.

3.4 Layer 3: Wireless Energy Fabric

The fabric comprises multiple WPT modalities, each with its domain of optimal operation:

Mode Technology Power Range Distance Governing Standard
A Inductive 5–50 W < 5 cm Qi2
B Resonant 50 W–2 kW < 30 cm SAE J2954
C RF 0.5–300 mW 0.1–3 m IEC TR 63377 (30 MHz–300 GHz)
D Beamformed RF 1–10 W 1–10 m IEC TR 63377
E Optical 1–100 W 1–100 m IEC 62471 (photobiological safety)

Table 1. WPT modalities and governing standards.

Remark 3.1 (Standards Mapping). IEC/IEEE 63184:2025 applies to inductive WPT in the range 3 kHz–30 MHz. It does not cover radiative WPT or optical WPT. IEC TR 63377:2022 covers radiative WPT in the range 30 MHz–300 GHz. Optical WPT requires photobiological safety assessment under IEC 62471. CEDA incorporates assessment frameworks consistent with each standard within its stated scope.

The mode selection function \mu: \mathcal{A} \to \{A, B, C, D, E\} maps an energy address to the optimal modality based on power, distance, safety, and efficiency criteria. Metasurfaces, where deployed, provide a programmable electromagnetic environment for beam focusing and steering; their efficiency gains and safety implications are treated as design parameters to be validated experimentally.

3.5 Layer 4: Energy Receivers (WER)

Each device contains a Wireless Energy Receiver (WER) comprising:

· Coupling element: coil (inductive/resonant) or antenna (RF/optical)
· Rectifier/DC-DC converter: for RF and resonant modes
· Authentication tag: cryptographic identity for energy addressing
· Feedback channel: for power negotiation and safety acknowledgment

The WER is characterized by its receiver profile:

\mathcal{R} = \left( \text{ID}, \mathbf{p}, P_{\min}, P_{\max}, \eta_{\text{rx}}, \mathcal{M}_{\text{supported}}, \mathcal{D}_{\text{class}} \right)

where \mathcal{M}_{\text{supported}} is the set of supported modalities and \mathcal{D}_{\text{class}} is the device class (including medical device flags).

3.6 Layer 5: Energy Control Plane

The control plane implements the energy routing and scheduling functions. Its principal components are:

3.6.1 Discovery and Localization. Receivers broadcast their identity and position. Transmitters use beacon signals to estimate direction and distance.

3.6.2 Authentication and Energy Handshake. Each receiver possesses a cryptographic identity. The energy handshake verifies:

· Authenticity of the receiver
· Authorization to draw power
· Compliance with safety constraints

The handshake is analogous to a TLS key exchange adapted to the energy domain. Mutual authentication prevents rogue devices from drawing power or interfering with authorized transfers.

3.6.3 Power Negotiation. The receiver requests P_{\text{req}}. The control plane computes the achievable power P_{\text{ach}} based on channel state, safety constraints, and resource availability. The negotiated power is:

P_{\text{neg}} = \min\left( P_{\text{req}}, P_{\text{ach}}, P_{\text{safe}} \right)

where P_{\text{safe}} is the maximum power compatible with the safety envelope.

3.6.4 Allocation and Scheduling. The allocation problem is solved as a constrained optimization (Section 2.5). Scheduling determines the time-division multiplexing of power delivery when simultaneous transfer would violate safety constraints.

3.6.5 Latency Budget. The control plane is separated into three temporal domains:

Domain Latency Target Function
Safety loop < 5 ms Hazard detection and shutdown
Control loop 100–200 ms Discovery, authentication, negotiation, allocation
Management loop seconds–minutes Scheduling, billing, optimization

Table 2. Control-plane temporal domains.

3.7 Layer 6: Safety Plane

The safety plane enforces the safety constraint vector (Section 2.4) through predictive envelope enforcement.

3.7.1 SAR Monitoring. Real-time SAR estimation is not achievable by external sensors alone. We adopt a two-step approach:

1. Pre-computed dosimetry database: FDTD simulations with anatomical human phantoms (e.g., Duke, Ella from the Virtual Family) at relevant frequencies, generating SAR distributions as a function of source position, power, and frequency.
2. Real-time external field monitoring: External E-field and H-field sensors measure the field at the boundary of the human-occupied region. The SAR estimate is obtained by coupling the measured fields to the pre-computed database.

The control plane reduces or ceases power delivery if:

\widehat{\text{SAR}}(\mathbf{x}, t) > \text{SAR}_{\lim}(f, \mathcal{H}) - \Delta_{\text{margin}}

3.7.2 Foreign Object Detection (FOD). Metallic objects in the transfer field cause eddy currents, heating, and efficiency loss. FOD is implemented via impedance monitoring (Q-factor degradation), thermal infrared sensing, and radar. Detection thresholds are specified in terms of minimum object size, minimum temperature rise, and response time. The system ceases power delivery within 5 ms of detection.

3.7.3 Medical Device Safety State (MDSS). IEC/IEEE 63184:2025 explicitly does not cover CIED immunity to WPT radiated disturbances. We therefore define a Medical Device Safety State:

D_{\text{medical}} = \left( \text{detection}, \text{confidence}, \text{location}, \text{device\_class}, \text{uncertainty} \right)

Detection is not equivalent to knowing implant type, location, immunity, and field susceptibility. We adopt a multi-layered approach:

1. Conservative exclusion zones with power reduction when a person is detected in the field (via radar, infrared, or capacitive sensing).
2. Magnetic field confinement to keep external fields below the ISO 14117 immunity threshold at the boundary of the exclusion zone.
3. User-declared medical status via smartphone app or wearable, integrated into the authentication handshake.
4. Fail-safe shutdown if field measurements exceed pre-computed safe thresholds.

The safe power constraint becomes:

P_{\text{safe}} = \min \left( P_{\text{human}}, P_{\text{thermal}}, P_{\text{FOD}}, P_{\text{CIED}}, P_{\text{system}} \right)

with P_{\text{CIED}} = 0 when the uncertainty in medical device presence exceeds a threshold.

3.7.4 Predictive Safety Enforcement. The safety plane does not wait for a violation. It predicts the future state and enforces:

\Pr \left[ \mathbf{s}(t + \Delta t) \notin \mathcal{S}_{\text{safe}} \mid \mathbf{s}(t), u(t) \right] \leq \epsilon

This transforms safety from reactive constraint checking to predictive constraint enforcement.

