Table 5. Standards mapping and scope.

Remark 5.1. CEDA does not claim compliance with any standard outside its stated frequency range or scope. IEC/IEEE 63184:2025 covers inductive WPT in 3 kHz–30 MHz and explicitly does not cover CIED immunity. IEC TR 63377:2022 covers radiative WPT in 30 MHz–300 GHz. Optical WPT requires photobiological safety assessment under IEC 62471.

5.2 CIED Safety

The system implements active CIED safety through exclusion zones, field confinement, and user-declared medical status. The magnetic field at any detected CIED location must remain below the immunity threshold specified in ISO 14117. Numerical studies of automotive WPT at 85 kHz show that the magnetic field inside the patient’s body is far below ICNIRP limits and the induced voltage is well below ISO 14117 immunity test levels for realistic exposure scenarios. However, these results are frequency- and geometry-specific and cannot be generalized without dosimetric validation.

5.3 Foreign Object Detection

Metallic objects in the transfer field are detected via impedance monitoring (Q-factor degradation), thermal infrared sensing, and radar. Detection thresholds are specified in terms of minimum object size, minimum temperature rise, and response time. The system ceases power delivery within 5 ms of detection.

