6. Feasibility Analysis

6.1 Energy Balance

For a typical residential building with P_{\text{peak}} = 15 kW and P_{\text{avg}} = 2.5 kW, the wireless fabric handles loads up to 2 kW per receiver. The storage system (10 kWh battery + 500 Wh supercapacitor) can supply the wireless fabric for approximately:

t_{\text{autonomy}} = \frac{E_{\text{st}} \cdot \eta_{\text{dis}}}{P_{\text{wireless}}} = \frac{10 \text{ kWh} \times 0.9}{2 \text{ kW}} = 4.5 \text{ hours}

which covers typical evening peak periods.

6.2 Virtualization Fraction as a Function of System Parameters

We replace the fixed 30–50% claim with a parameterized bound. The virtualization fraction \alpha^\star depends on:

· Maximum wireless power P_{\text{th}}
· Maximum practical distance d_{\text{th}}
· End-to-end efficiency \eta
· Safety margins
· Occupancy patterns
· Cost parameters

Proposition 6.1. For a typical residential load profile with P_{\text{th}} = 2 kW and d_{\text{th}} = 30 cm, and assuming 90% end-to-end efficiency, the energy-based virtualization fraction satisfies \alpha^\star \leq 0.40, with the upper bound achieved only under favorable occupancy and safety conditions. The lower bound depends on the fraction of loads that are safety-constrained (e.g., near medical devices) or economically unfavorable.

This proposition is to be tested through simulation and, ultimately, experimental validation. We do not assert a fixed number.

6.3 Economic Considerations

The economic viability of CEDA depends on:

· Cost of Energy Nodes
· Cost of WERs
· Installation cost compared to conventional wiring
· Operational savings from reduced wiring maintenance and increased flexibility

For new construction, CEDA may achieve cost parity with conventional wiring within a defined deployment horizon. Retrofit applications are less favorable due to the need for storage installation and node placement. A full techno-economic analysis is identified as future work.

---

7. Conclusion and Future Work

This dissertation has established the theoretical foundations for a building-scale cableless energy distribution architecture. The key findings are:

1. The physical transfer mechanisms exist. Resonant, RF, and optical WPT modalities are mature enough to support a multi-mode energy fabric, with benchmarks from SAE J2954 and recent rectenna literature.
2. The architectural gap is real. No existing system integrates storage, multi-mode transfer, spatial addressing, authentication, and safety governance at the building scale.
3. The virtualization bound is finite and parameterized. We have replaced the fixed 30–50% claim with a constrained optimization problem. The achievable fraction depends on power, distance, efficiency, safety, occupancy, and cost.
4. Safety is the binding constraint. SAR limits, CIED immunity, and foreign-object detection must be enforced by a dedicated safety plane with predictive envelope enforcement and millisecond-scale shutdown.
5. Standards compliance is scope-limited. CEDA incorporates assessment frameworks consistent with IEC/IEEE 63184:2025 for inductive WPT and IEC TR 63377:2022 for radiative WPT, within their stated frequency ranges.

Future work will address:

· Experimental validation of the hybrid architecture in a controlled testbed
· FDTD dosimetry with anatomical phantoms for SAR assessment
· Multi-receiver allocation simulator with SCA and convex optimization
· Formal safety case for regulatory approval
· Extension to commercial and industrial building scales
· Integration with demand-response and grid-interactive energy management
· Refinement of the energy addressing protocol for interoperability
· Techno-economic analysis with real building energy data

The transformation of electrical energy from a physically distributed resource into an addressable, routable, and safety-governed fabric is not a question of if, but of whether and under what constraints. This dissertation provides the theoretical framework for that transformation, with explicit acknowledgment of what is demonstrated, what is hypothesized, and what remains to be validated.

