Appendix A: Mathematical Derivations and Proofs

A.1 Coupled-Mode Theory for Multi-Receiver Resonant WPT

A.1.1 Full Impedance Matrix Derivation

We consider a resonant WPT system with N_T transmitters and N_R receivers. Each transmitter i is characterized by self-inductance L_{T,i}, compensation capacitance C_{T,i}, and parasitic resistance R_{T,i}. Each receiver j is characterized by self-inductance L_{R,j}, compensation capacitance C_{R,j}, and load resistance R_{L,j}. The mutual inductance between transmitter i and receiver j is M_{ij}. The mutual inductance between receivers j and k is M_{jk}^{(R)}, which is non-negligible in multi-receiver systems and induces frequency-splitting behavior through cross-coupling paths.

The coupled-mode equations in the frequency domain are:

\begin{pmatrix}
\mathbf{Z}_T & j\omega\mathbf{M}_{TR} \\
j\omega\mathbf{M}_{TR}^\top & \mathbf{Z}_R
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
\mathbf{I}_T \\
\mathbf{I}_R
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
\mathbf{V}_s \\
\mathbf{0}
\end{pmatrix}
\tag{A.1}

where:

· \mathbf{Z}_T \in \mathbb{C}^{N_T \times N_T} is the transmitter impedance matrix with diagonal elements Z_{T,ii} = R_{T,i} + j\omega L_{T,i} + 1/(j\omega C_{T,i}) and off-diagonal elements Z_{T,ik} = j\omega M_{ik}^{(T)} (transmitter-to-transmitter mutual coupling, usually negligible);
· \mathbf{Z}_R \in \mathbb{C}^{N_R \times N_R} is the receiver impedance matrix with diagonal elements Z_{R,jj} = R_{L,j} + R_{R,j} + j\omega L_{R,j} + 1/(j\omega C_{R,j}) and off-diagonal elements Z_{R,jk} = j\omega M_{jk}^{(R)};
· \mathbf{M}_{TR} \in \mathbb{R}^{N_T \times N_R} is the transmitter-receiver mutual inductance matrix;
· \mathbf{V}_s \in \mathbb{C}^{N_T} is the source voltage vector.

Remark A.1. The receiver cross-coupling terms M_{jk}^{(R)} are often neglected in simplified analyses, but they are physically significant in dense multi-receiver deployments. As demonstrated in recent work on tri-coil dual-load MCR-WPT systems, cross-coupling between receivers induces local modal reconfiguration and energy redistribution, leading to secondary or minor characteristic peaks in the frequency response.

A.1.2 Elimination of Receiver Currents

From the second block row of (A.1):

j\omega\mathbf{M}_{TR}^\top \mathbf{I}_T + \mathbf{Z}_R \mathbf{I}_R = \mathbf{0}
\tag{A.2}

Solving for \mathbf{I}_R:

\mathbf{I}_R = -\mathbf{Z}_R^{-1} (j\omega\mathbf{M}_{TR}^\top) \mathbf{I}_T
\tag{A.3}

Substituting into the first block row:

\mathbf{Z}_T \mathbf{I}_T + j\omega\mathbf{M}_{TR} \mathbf{I}_R = \mathbf{V}_s
\tag{A.4}

\left[ \mathbf{Z}_T - \omega^2 \mathbf{M}_{TR} \mathbf{Z}_R^{-1} \mathbf{M}_{TR}^\top \right] \mathbf{I}_T = \mathbf{V}_s
\tag{A.5}

Define the effective transmitter impedance matrix:

\mathbf{Z}_{\text{eff}} = \mathbf{Z}_T - \omega^2 \mathbf{M}_{TR} \mathbf{Z}_R^{-1} \mathbf{M}_{TR}^\top
\tag{A.6}

Then:

\mathbf{I}_T = \mathbf{Z}_{\text{eff}}^{-1} \mathbf{V}_s
\tag{A.7}

A.1.3 Power Delivered to Each Receiver

The power delivered to receiver j is:

P_j = \frac{1}{2} \text{Re}\left( \mathbf{I}_{R,j}^H \mathbf{Z}_{L,j} \mathbf{I}_{R,j} \right)
\tag{A.8}

where \mathbf{Z}_{L,j} is the load impedance of receiver j. Substituting (A.3):

P_j = \frac{1}{2} \text{Re}\left( \mathbf{I}_T^H \mathbf{M}_{TR}^\top \mathbf{Z}_R^{-H} \mathbf{Z}_{L,j} \mathbf{Z}_R^{-1} \mathbf{M}_{TR}^\top \mathbf{I}_T \right)
\tag{A.9}

Proposition A.1 (Convexity of Power Allocation with Fixed Loads). Consider the power allocation problem:

\max_{\mathbf{I}_T} \sum_{j=1}^{N_R} w_j P_j
\tag{A.10}

subject to:

P_j \geq P_{\min,j} \quad \forall j
\tag{A.11}

\|\mathbf{I}_{T,i}\| \leq I_{\max} \quad \forall i
\tag{A.12}

where P_j is given by (A.9) and the mutual inductance matrix \mathbf{M}_{TR} is fixed. Under the assumption of linear receiver loads (fixed \mathbf{Z}_R), the objective is a convex quadratic form in \mathbf{I}_T, and the feasible set defined by (A.11)–(A.12) is convex. Therefore, the problem admits a global maximum that can be found by standard convex optimization techniques.

Proof. The power delivered to receiver j can be written as:

P_j = \frac{1}{2} \mathbf{I}_T^H \mathbf{Q}_j \mathbf{I}_T
\tag{A.13}

where:

\mathbf{Q}_j = \mathbf{M}_{TR} \mathbf{Z}_R^{-H} \mathbf{Z}_{L,j} \mathbf{Z}_R^{-1} \mathbf{M}_{TR}^\top
\tag{A.14}

Since \mathbf{Z}_{L,j} is positive definite (load resistance is positive), and \mathbf{Z}_R is invertible, the matrix \mathbf{Q}_j is positive semidefinite. Therefore, P_j is a convex function of \mathbf{I}_T. The objective in (A.10) is a non-negative weighted sum of convex functions, hence convex. The constraints (A.11) define a convex set (superlevel sets of convex functions), and (A.12) defines a convex set (norm balls). The intersection of convex sets is convex, and maximizing a convex function over a convex set is a convex optimization problem. \square

Remark A.2. For nonlinear rectenna loads, the problem becomes non-convex. In that case, successive convex approximation (SCA) is adopted, with convergence guarantees under mild regularity conditions.

A.1.4 Efficiency Formula Derivation

For a single transmitter-receiver pair, the coil-to-coil efficiency is:

\eta_{\text{coil}} = \frac{P_L}{P_{\text{in}}}
\tag{A.15}

At resonance, with the receiver tuned to the same frequency as the transmitter:

\eta_{\text{coil}} = \frac{k^2 Q_t Q_r}{\left(1 + \sqrt{1 + k^2 Q_t Q_r}\right)^2}
\tag{A.16}

where Q_t = \omega_0 L_1 / R_1 and Q_r = \omega_0 L_2 / R_2.

Proof. The efficiency of a two-coil resonant WPT system can be derived from the coupled-mode equations. Let a_1 and a_2 be the mode amplitudes of the transmitter and receiver resonators:

\frac{da_1}{dt} = (j\omega_0 - \Gamma_1) a_1 + j\kappa a_2 + s
\tag{A.17}

\frac{da_2}{dt} = (j\omega_0 - \Gamma_2 - \Gamma_L) a_2 + j\kappa a_1
\tag{A.18}

where \Gamma_1 = R_1/(2L_1), \Gamma_2 = R_2/(2L_2), \Gamma_L = R_L/(2L_2), and \kappa = \omega_0 k / 2.

In steady state (d/dt = 0):

(j\Delta\omega + \Gamma_1) a_1 - j\kappa a_2 = s
\tag{A.19}

-j\kappa a_1 + (j\Delta\omega + \Gamma_2 + \Gamma_L) a_2 = 0
\tag{A.20}

At resonance (\Delta\omega = 0):

a_2 = \frac{j\kappa}{\Gamma_2 + \Gamma_L} a_1
\tag{A.21}

The efficiency is:

\eta = \frac{\Gamma_L |a_2|^2}{\Gamma_1 |a_1|^2 + (\Gamma_2 + \Gamma_L) |a_2|^2}
\tag{A.22}

Substituting (A.21) and using the definitions of Q and k:

\eta = \frac{k^2 Q_t Q_r}{\left(1 + \sqrt{1 + k^2 Q_t Q_r}\right)^2}
\tag{A.23}

\square

Proposition A.2 (System Efficiency Cascade). The system-level efficiency is:

\eta_{\text{system}} = \eta_{\text{inverter}} \cdot \eta_{\text{coil}} \cdot \eta_{\text{rectifier}} \cdot \eta_{\text{DC-DC}}
\tag{A.24}

For SAE J2954-compliant systems, \eta_{\text{system}} \geq 0.85 at nominal alignment.

