A.2 Radiative WPT: Friis Equation and Rectenna Efficiency

A.2.1 Friis Transmission Equation

The received power at distance d from a transmitter with EIRP P_{\text{EIRP}} and receiver antenna gain G_r is:

P_r(d) = P_{\text{EIRP}} \cdot G_r \cdot \left(\frac{\lambda}{4\pi d}\right)^2
\tag{A.25}

A.2.2 Rectenna Efficiency Model

The DC output power is:

P_{\text{DC}} = \eta_{\text{rect}}(P_r) \cdot P_r
\tag{A.26}

Recent rectenna designs demonstrate 80% RF-to-DC conversion efficiency at 2.45 GHz under 10 dBm input power, with efficiency maintained above 60% across load impedances from 1000~\Omega to 3000~\Omega. A measured rectenna achieved 38% efficiency at 2.3 dBm input power and 1.5~\text{k}\Omega load. These benchmarks inform the efficiency range used in CEDA simulations.

A.2.3 Beamforming Array Gain

For an N_T-element array, the array gain is:

G_{\text{array}} = N_T \cdot G_{\text{element}}
\tag{A.27}

with phase shifts:

\phi_n = -\frac{2\pi}{\lambda} \mathbf{r}_n \cdot \hat{\mathbf{u}}(\theta, \phi)
\tag{A.28}

Remark A.3 (Mutual Coupling Correction). For dense arrays, mutual coupling between elements reduces the effective gain by a factor \Gamma_{\text{cpl}}. The corrected array gain is:

G_{\text{array}}^{\text{eff}} = \Gamma_{\text{cpl}} \cdot N_T \cdot G_{\text{element}}
\tag{A.29}

