A.3 Safety Constraint Vector Formalism

A.3.1 Full Constraint Vector Derivation

The safety constraint vector is:

\mathcal{S}_{\text{safe}}(f, \mathbf{x}, t, \mathcal{H}, \mathcal{D}) =
\begin{pmatrix}
\text{SAR}(f, \mathbf{x}, t) - \text{SAR}_{\lim}(f, \mathcal{H}) \\
|\mathbf{E}_{\text{int}}(f, \mathbf{x}, t)| - E_{\lim}(f) \\
|\mathbf{J}(f, \mathbf{x}, t)| - J_{\lim}(f) \\
B_{\text{ext}}(f, \mathbf{x}, t) - B_{\text{CIED}} \\
T(\mathbf{x}, t) - T_{\text{safe}} \\
P_{\text{contact}}(t) - P_{\text{contact,lim}}
\end{pmatrix}
\leq \mathbf{0}
\tag{A.30}

Derivation of SAR Constraint. SAR is defined as:

\text{SAR} = \frac{\sigma |\mathbf{E}|^2}{2\rho}
\tag{A.31}

For peak amplitude, SAR = \sigma |\mathbf{E}|^2 / (2\rho). For RMS, SAR = \sigma |\mathbf{E}|_{\text{rms}}^2 / \rho. The spatially averaged SAR over a region V is:

\text{SAR}_{\text{avg}} = \frac{1}{V} \int_V \frac{\sigma |\mathbf{E}(\mathbf{x})|^2}{2\rho} \, d^3\mathbf{x}
\tag{A.32}

ICNIRP 2020 specifies a peak spatial-averaged 10 g SAR limit of 2 W/kg for general public exposure between 100 kHz and 6 GHz. IEEE C95.1-2019 covers 0 Hz–300 GHz and explicitly states that its exposure limits “might not be protective with respect to the use of medical devices or implants.”

Derivation of CIED Constraint. ISO 14117 assumes that the CIED lead loop is planar, semicircular, and has an area of 225 cm². The induced voltage at the pacemaker input port is:

V_{\text{ind}} = -j\omega \oint_{\text{loop}} \mathbf{B} \cdot d\mathbf{A}
\tag{A.33}

For a uniform magnetic field B perpendicular to the loop:

|V_{\text{ind}}| = \omega B A_{\text{loop}}
\tag{A.34}

The ISO 14117 immunity threshold for CIEDs is defined in terms of the induced voltage. SAE J2954:2024 specifies an emission limit where the 4-point averaged measurements must not exceed a CIED reference level of 15 μT.

Proposition A.3 (Safety Envelope Invariance). The safety envelope \mathcal{S}_{\text{safe}} is invariant under the transformation \mathbf{s} \mapsto \mathbf{s} + \mathbf{c} if and only if \mathbf{c} \leq \mathbf{0}. This follows directly from the definition of the feasible set as a superlevel set of convex constraints.

A.3.2 Predictive Safety Enforcement

The predictive safety constraint is:

\Pr \left[ \mathbf{s}(t + \Delta t) \notin \mathcal{S}_{\text{safe}} \mid \mathbf{s}(t), u(t) \right] \leq \epsilon
\tag{A.35}

For a linear system with Gaussian noise:

\mathbf{s}(t + \Delta t) = \mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}(t) + \mathbf{B}u(t) + \mathbf{w}(t)
\tag{A.36}

where \mathbf{w}(t) \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, \mathbf{W}). The safety probability constraint can be approximated using the Mahalanobis distance:

(\mathbf{s}(t + \Delta t) - \boldsymbol{\mu})^\top \mathbf{\Sigma}^{-1} (\mathbf{s}(t + \Delta t) - \boldsymbol{\mu}) \leq \chi^2_{n, 1-\epsilon}
\tag{A.37}

where \boldsymbol{\mu} = \mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}(t) + \mathbf{B}u(t) and \mathbf{\Sigma} = \mathbf{W}.

