A.4 Virtualization Fraction Optimization

A.4.1 Formal Problem Statement

\alpha^\star = \max_{\pi \in \Pi} \frac{\sum_{l \in \mathcal{L}} E_l \cdot v_l(\pi)}{\sum_{l \in \mathcal{L}} E_l}
\tag{A.38}

subject to:

v_l(\pi) = 0 \quad \text{if } P_l > P_{\text{th}} \text{ or } d_l > d_{\text{th}} \text{ or } \mathcal{S}_{\text{safe}} \not\leq \mathbf{0}
\tag{A.39}

v_l(\pi) \in [0,1] \quad \forall l
\tag{A.40}

\sum_{l} P_{\text{del}, l}(\pi) \leq P_{\text{fabric}} \quad \forall t
\tag{A.41}

A.4.2 Decomposition by Load Category

For a residential building, the load set partitions into:

\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_{\text{high}} \cup \mathcal{L}_{\text{medium}} \cup \mathcal{L}_{\text{low}} \cup \mathcal{L}_{\text{IoT}}
\tag{A.42}

Category Typical Power Typical Energy Wirelessly Serviceable?
High (cooking, heating, EV) 2–22 kW 30–50% No (P > P_{\text{th}})
Medium (appliances, robotics) 100–500 W 15–25% Yes
Low (lighting, electronics) 10–100 W 10–20% Yes
IoT (sensors, embedded) 0.1–10 W <5% Yes

Proposition A.4 (Upper Bound on Virtualization Fraction). Under the assumption that high-power loads (P_l > P_{\text{th}}) cannot be served wirelessly, the virtualization fraction satisfies:

\alpha^\star \leq 1 - \frac{\sum_{l \in \mathcal{L}_{\text{high}}} E_l}{\sum_{l \in \mathcal{L}} E_l}
\tag{A.43}

For a typical residential load profile where \mathcal{L}_{\text{high}} accounts for 40–60% of daily energy, \alpha^\star \leq 0.60 by energy.

Proposition A.5 (Lower Bound Under Safety Constraints). If a fraction \beta of the medium- and low-power loads are safety-constrained (e.g., near medical devices or in exclusion zones), then:

\alpha^\star \geq (1 - \beta) \cdot \frac{\sum_{l \in \mathcal{L}_{\text{medium}} \cup \mathcal{L}_{\text{low}} \cup \mathcal{L}_{\text{IoT}}} E_l}{\sum_{l \in \mathcal{L}} E_l}
\tag{A.44}

For \beta = 0.3, this gives a lower bound of approximately 0.15–0.25.

