Appendix C: Safety Analysis and Dosimetry Methodology

C.1 FDTD Dosimetry Framework

C.1.1 Anatomical Human Phantoms

SAR assessment in CEDA uses the Virtual Family anatomical models (Duke, Ella) and the Korean human model, with frequency-dependent tissue properties. The FDTD method is employed for computing internal electric fields and SAR distributions.

C.1.2 Computational Parameters

Parameter Value
Grid resolution 1–2 mm (at 85 kHz); 0.5–1 mm (at 2.4 GHz)
Tissue properties IT'IS Database (frequency-dependent)
Boundary conditions PML (perfectly matched layers)
Source model Equivalent current source from coil geometry
SAR averaging 10 g (ICNIRP) and 1 g (IEEE C95.1)

C.1.3 Pre-Computed Dosimetry Database

The database stores SAR distributions as a function of:

· Source position (\mathbf{p}_s)
· Source orientation (\mathbf{R}_s)
· Frequency f
· Transmitter power P_{\text{Tx}}

For real-time operation, the SAR at any point is approximated by interpolation:

\widehat{\text{SAR}}(\mathbf{x}, t) = \sum_{k} w_k(\mathbf{x}, t) \cdot \text{SAR}_k
\tag{C.1}

where w_k are interpolation weights determined by the current source configuration.

C.2 CIED Safety Assessment

C.2.1 Induced Voltage Computation

The induced voltage at the CIED lead input is computed using the ISO 14117 lead loop model:

V_{\text{ind}} = -j\omega \int_{\text{loop}} \mathbf{B} \cdot d\mathbf{A}
\tag{C.2}

For a uniform field:

|V_{\text{ind}}| = \omega B A_{\text{loop}}
\tag{C.3}

where A_{\text{loop}} = 225~\text{cm}^2.

C.2.2 SAE J2954 CIED Reference Level

SAE J2954:2024 specifies that the 4-point averaged magnetic field measurements must not exceed a CIED reference level of 15 μT. CEDA adopts this as the CIED constraint:

B_{\text{ext}}(f, \mathbf{x}, t) \leq 15~\mu\text{T}
\tag{C.4}

at any location where a CIED may be present.

