\mathcal{S}_{\text{safe}}(f, \mathbf{x}, t, \mathcal{H}, \mathcal{D}) \leq \mathbf{0}
\tag{E.3}

\|\mathbf{I}_T^{(n)}\| \leq I_{\max} \quad \forall n
\tag{E.4}

\sum_{n} P_{\text{Tx}}^{(n)} \leq P_{\text{budget}}
\tag{E.5}

E.1.2 Solution Algorithm

For linear loads, the problem is convex (Proposition A.1) and solved using interior-point methods. For nonlinear rectenna loads, successive convex approximation (SCA) is employed with the following iteration:

1. Initialize \mathbf{I}_T^{(0)}
2. At iteration k, linearize the non-convex constraints around \mathbf{I}_T^{(k)}
3. Solve the convex subproblem to obtain \mathbf{I}_T^{(k+1)}
4. Repeat until convergence

E.2 FDTD Dosimetry Validation

Simulations are performed using the finite-difference time-domain method with anatomical phantoms. The SAR distribution is computed and compared with published benchmarks for WPT systems. For a magnetic resonance WPT system at 85 kHz, the maximum 10 g SAR in the human thoracic region is approximately 0.142–0.148 W/kg, well below the ICNIRP limit of 2 W/kg.

E.3 Virtualization Fraction Simulation

The virtualization fraction \alpha^\star is computed for a typical residential load profile with the following parameters:

Parameter Value
P_{\text{th}} 2 kW
d_{\text{th}} 30 cm (resonant), 3 m (RF)
\eta_{\text{system}} 0.85 (resonant), 0.60 (RF)
High-power load fraction 45% of energy
Medium/low-power load fraction 40% of energy
Safety-constrained fraction 15%

Under these assumptions, the simulation yields \alpha^\star \approx 0.25–0.35 by energy, consistent with the bounds derived in Appendix A.4.

